course: dictionary
lesson: 2701
title: Digestion: breaking food down
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Digestion is the process in your body that breaks food down into tiny parts that your body can take in and use. A whole sandwich is too big for your body to use straight away, so it must be broken down first.
explain: «Digestion starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva.» Your teeth break food into smaller bits, and saliva, the wet liquid in your mouth, starts to soften it. Then the food travels down the gullet to the stomach, and digestion carries on.
explain: Digestion can also mean the body's ability to do this job. Fibre, water and exercise help good digestion, so looking after yourself helps your body break food down well. You will also hear the phrases «the digestive system» and «aid digestion».
fact: Digestion means the process that breaks food down into tiny parts the body can take in and use.
fact: Food goes from the mouth, down the gullet, to the stomach, and then into the small intestine.
fact: «The small intestine is about 6 to 7 metres long in an adult, folded up in the tummy, and most digestion and absorption of food into the blood happens there.»
fact: The large intestine takes water back into the body, and what is left leaves the body as poo.
fact: Fibre, water and exercise help good digestion.
exercise: «Digestion starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva.» Where does digestion start? | In the large intestine | In the stomach | In the small intestine | *In the mouth
exercise: «The small intestine is about 6 to 7 metres long in an adult... most digestion and absorption of food into the blood happens there.» Where does most digestion happen? | In the mouth | *In the small intestine | In the gullet | Outside the body
exercise: Which of these helps good digestion? | Sitting still all day | Never drinking water | *Fibre, water and exercise | Eating only sweets
test: After the mouth, food travels down the gullet. Where does it go next? | *The stomach | The brain | The heart | The large intestine
test: Water is taken back into the body in the large intestine. What leaves the body at the end of digestion? | Water | *Poo, the food that is left over | Saliva | Blood
exam: Digestion is best described as: | *The process that breaks food down so the body can take it in and use it | Storing heat under the skin | A way of breathing air into the lungs | Growing bones and muscles
